  "source": "Written for Loppa (IMPORT-PIPELINE.md §2.6 and §2.11). Page-number keys are in their folded form: NFKC, lower case, every digit run as #, whitespace collapsed. Conjunctions are the words a suspended compound's hyphen stands before (för- och efternamn, Ein- und Ausgang, pre- and post-war), in lower case. Stop words are frequent function words of each language, chosen so the language's own words outnumber its neighbours' on ordinary prose; Chinese and Japanese are read a character at a time, so theirs are single characters and Japanese's are kana. A word as common in one language as in another is listed for both, so it tells neither apart: \"ja\" is \"and\" in Finnish and \"yes\" in Swedish, Danish, Norwegian and German, where forms print it beside every box; \"no\" is English as well as Spanish; \"bli\" Swedish as well as Norwegian; \"um\" German as well as Icelandic; and \"in\" German and Swedish as well as English (CAVEATS #162).",
